WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.369 --> 00:00:02.687
(Garrett inhales deeply)
- Ah, nature. Hi. I'm Garrett.

00:00:02.687 --> 00:00:03.520
- And I'm Niki.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.194
- We're fascinated by
how other people live.

00:00:05.194 --> 00:00:06.027
- [Niki] So we're gonna try to find

00:00:06.027 --> 00:00:07.669
some really interesting living situations.

00:00:07.669 --> 00:00:08.502
- [Garrett] And live there,

00:00:08.502 --> 00:00:10.262
experiencing a totally
different lifestyle.

00:00:10.262 --> 00:00:11.931
- [Niki] To see which ones
will bring us closer together.

00:00:11.931 --> 00:00:13.437
- [Garrett] Or push us farther apart.

00:00:13.437 --> 00:00:16.743
- This week, we're livin' on a ranch!

00:00:16.743 --> 00:00:18.087
This is what ranches look like.

00:00:18.087 --> 00:00:20.469
- It's thousands of acres.
- It's huge.

00:00:20.469 --> 00:00:22.802
(hip muzak)

00:00:23.962 --> 00:00:27.129
(upbeat guitar music)

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:31.515
- I'm Jeanne.
- I'm Eric.

00:00:31.515 --> 00:00:33.022
- [Jeanne] And we're here
at the Cold Creek Ranch,

00:00:33.022 --> 00:00:35.468
and we've been on this ranch for 12 years.

00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:39.018
- [Eric] This idea here is
to apply everything we know

00:00:39.018 --> 00:00:41.750
to manage the land for its best health.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.235
Not for the cattle's health.
For the land's health.

00:00:44.235 --> 00:00:45.965
- And then the cattle's
health will follow.

00:00:45.965 --> 00:00:47.584
- Lifestyle on a ranch, to me,

00:00:47.584 --> 00:00:49.291
sounds like you're wearing cowboy boots

00:00:49.291 --> 00:00:50.899
and you're using those
cowboy boots to step

00:00:50.899 --> 00:00:52.578
in horseshit all day long.

00:00:52.578 --> 00:00:54.990
- I've never lived in
any sort of ranch or farm

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:57.994
or wild countryside in the Wild Wild West.

00:00:57.994 --> 00:01:00.147
Wild.
(laughs)

00:01:00.147 --> 00:01:02.014
- [Jeanne] We're our own water company,

00:01:02.014 --> 00:01:03.543
our own electric company.

00:01:03.543 --> 00:01:06.071
Only one part of the ranch
has real electricity.

00:01:06.071 --> 00:01:08.843
- [Eric] A typical day could
start out shoeing a horse,

00:01:08.843 --> 00:01:11.858
and because of something
happening two hours later,

00:01:11.858 --> 00:01:13.608
you might be repairing a water line.

00:01:13.608 --> 00:01:15.358
It's all over the place all the time.

00:01:15.358 --> 00:01:16.981
- All I know about
ranches is what I've seen

00:01:16.981 --> 00:01:18.042
on TV and movies.

00:01:18.042 --> 00:01:19.434
Not a great frame of reference.

00:01:19.434 --> 00:01:20.820
I feel like I'm gonna meet a farmhand

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:22.906
and fall in love with him
while he carries hay bales,

00:01:22.906 --> 00:01:26.384
and I sit inside drinking tea
in a ridiculous pink dress.

00:01:26.384 --> 00:01:29.167
- A lot of people get excited
about ranches. I don't get it.

00:01:29.167 --> 00:01:31.735
I don't think horses are cool.
I don't think cows are cool.

00:01:31.735 --> 00:01:33.108
I don't like the idea of ranches.

00:01:33.108 --> 00:01:34.923
I like barbecue.

00:01:34.923 --> 00:01:36.569
- Look at this beautiful door.

00:01:36.569 --> 00:01:38.130
Is that a horse foot knocker?

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:38.963
- Yes.
- Yep.

00:01:38.963 --> 00:01:39.796
- [Niki] Use it.

00:01:39.796 --> 00:01:41.308
- [Eric] That's actually Trampus' foot.

00:01:41.308 --> 00:01:43.487
- Niki and I, we live in
a lot of different places

00:01:43.487 --> 00:01:46.120
that all feel like the 21st Century.

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.161
This feels like it's from the late 1800s.

00:01:49.161 --> 00:01:50.685
That wood-burning stove
in there is amazing.

00:01:50.685 --> 00:01:52.551
- [Eric] This is the only heat we've got.

00:01:52.551 --> 00:01:53.384
- [Niki] Oh, my goodness.

00:01:53.384 --> 00:01:55.772
- We only have this house about
three week out of the year

00:01:55.772 --> 00:01:57.788
and the rest of the time
you're trying to cool it off.

00:01:57.788 --> 00:01:59.964
- I've lived my entire life in cities.

00:01:59.964 --> 00:02:02.267
This is gonna be a very,
very different lifestyle.

00:02:02.267 --> 00:02:03.377
I'm pretty scared.

00:02:03.377 --> 00:02:04.870
- [Eric] This is the living room,

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:07.470
although there's gotta be a
better name for it than that.

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:08.866
We're turning it into a library.

00:02:08.866 --> 00:02:11.150
The wood work is all made by local joiners

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:13.495
who really know their craft.

00:02:13.495 --> 00:02:16.284
It's hard to join something
that's not straight.

00:02:16.284 --> 00:02:18.086
I'm gonna show you the way upstairs.

00:02:18.086 --> 00:02:19.798
- [Jeanne] We have the Western
Room and the Africa Room.

00:02:19.798 --> 00:02:21.374
You guys can choose. Flip a coin.

00:02:21.374 --> 00:02:23.426
Whatever you want to do to
decide who gets which room.

00:02:23.426 --> 00:02:25.273
- Do they join?
- Yes, they do.

00:02:25.273 --> 00:02:27.044
- There's a shared
bathroom in between them.

00:02:27.044 --> 00:02:28.101
- This is perfect.

00:02:28.101 --> 00:02:30.559
- So this is the Africa Room.
- Wow.

00:02:30.559 --> 00:02:32.356
- [Jeanne] We have spent
a lot of time in Africa.

00:02:32.356 --> 00:02:34.268
- To be honest, that thing
freaks me out a little bit.

00:02:34.268 --> 00:02:37.318
I mean, it's very beautiful, but...

00:02:37.318 --> 00:02:38.271
- The eyeballs.
- Yeah.

00:02:38.271 --> 00:02:39.859
- So you can have this room.
- That's fine.

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:40.935
- I think this is gonna require

00:02:40.935 --> 00:02:43.069
a lot of early mornings and labor.

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:47.230
I am probably gonna look
like a real stupid city kid.

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:49.896
I'm not gonna know how anything works.

00:02:49.896 --> 00:02:51.412
- I'm really nervous about this ranch.

00:02:51.412 --> 00:02:52.837
Hopefully we can rely on each other

00:02:52.837 --> 00:02:54.494
instead of feeling
burdened by one another.

00:02:54.494 --> 00:02:56.837
- I don't think it will bode
well for our relationship.

00:02:56.837 --> 00:02:59.991
- Well we're gonna head on
up the telephone trailer.

00:02:59.991 --> 00:03:02.005
This is something I do usually once a day

00:03:02.005 --> 00:03:03.837
because there is no cell phone coverage

00:03:03.837 --> 00:03:05.297
down at the bottom of this canyon.

00:03:05.297 --> 00:03:06.745
- So many people now think of a landscape

00:03:06.745 --> 00:03:08.379
as just sort of a backdrop.

00:03:08.379 --> 00:03:10.640
It has nothing to do with
anything they do in their lives,

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.355
and they come away from here generally

00:03:12.355 --> 00:03:15.779
with a very, very vivid
idea of just how involved

00:03:15.779 --> 00:03:17.179
we are with our landscape.

00:03:17.179 --> 00:03:20.315
It's a lifestyle. It's not
a career in that sense.

00:03:20.315 --> 00:03:23.273
- [Garrett] It blew my
mind that you could own

00:03:23.273 --> 00:03:25.650
all of the land to,
basically, the horizon.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:27.737
- [Niki] We got to see
as much of the property

00:03:27.737 --> 00:03:30.288
as we could from one vantage
point, which is crazy

00:03:30.288 --> 00:03:32.816
because that's something
like ten thousand acres.

00:03:32.816 --> 00:03:34.063
- They only have to conserve
electricity at night

00:03:34.063 --> 00:03:35.883
for six weeks out of the year.

00:03:35.883 --> 00:03:38.736
And we just happen to be here
during one of those weeks.

00:03:38.736 --> 00:03:40.139
- [Niki] We cooked dinner in the dark.

00:03:40.139 --> 00:03:42.253
Which was a little scary because
I was working with knives,

00:03:42.253 --> 00:03:44.341
but still have all my fingers.

00:03:44.341 --> 00:03:46.044
Tomorrow should be a full day of work,

00:03:46.044 --> 00:03:48.840
and it's really whatever the ranch needs

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.677
taken care of tomorrow is
whatever we will tend to.

00:03:51.677 --> 00:03:54.516
- [Garrett] Let's go
say goodnight to Niki.

00:03:54.516 --> 00:03:57.308
Okay, goodnight Niki.
- Goodnight.

00:03:57.308 --> 00:04:00.051
- Sleep tight. I'm just gonna be...

00:04:00.051 --> 00:04:01.997
- Right over there.
(they laugh)

00:04:01.997 --> 00:04:05.997
- In there. With my door
open so the heat stays.

00:04:07.101 --> 00:04:09.626
- Goodnight.
- Goodnight.

00:04:09.626 --> 00:04:10.459
(loud bang)

00:04:10.459 --> 00:04:11.883
- Ooh, god.
- Ooh. Are you okay?

00:04:11.883 --> 00:04:13.749
- No, it wasn't my head.
That was the thing.

00:04:13.749 --> 00:04:15.720
- Okay. (laughs)
- I'm fine.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.120
(bright string music)

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.453
- It's about seven-ish AM,
and I think it's raining.

00:04:23.492 --> 00:04:25.448
So I don't know what that means for today,

00:04:25.448 --> 00:04:28.170
but we might have to change our plans.

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:30.176
- Good morning, guys.
- Morning.

00:04:30.176 --> 00:04:31.462
- Thank you for breakfast.
- You're welcome.

00:04:31.462 --> 00:04:32.463
- [Niki] Thank you so much for breakfast.

00:04:32.463 --> 00:04:34.587
- [Garrett] There are the solar panels.

00:04:34.587 --> 00:04:37.278
They're not gonna get
much electricity today.

00:04:37.278 --> 00:04:39.748
- We've made some alternative arrangements

00:04:39.748 --> 00:04:43.448
depending on how up to learning
how to shoe a horse you are.

00:04:43.448 --> 00:04:45.094
- How many horses did
you say you got up here?

00:04:45.094 --> 00:04:46.530
- [Eric] There's four in here now,

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:48.324
but we got three more up on top

00:04:48.324 --> 00:04:49.990
and then a whole bunch of strays

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:52.448
over on the other side of the ranch.

00:04:52.448 --> 00:04:53.847
- Get outta here. Leave me alone.

00:04:53.847 --> 00:04:56.263
- Garrett's taller than the horse.

00:04:56.263 --> 00:04:57.657
- I'm gonna feed ya.

00:04:57.657 --> 00:04:59.082
- [Niki] We had to switch to plan b

00:04:59.082 --> 00:05:00.853
which involved taking care of the horses.

00:05:00.853 --> 00:05:02.089
That was kind of an opportunity

00:05:02.089 --> 00:05:04.350
that Eric and Jeanne had to do tasks

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:06.527
that they don't usually have time to do.

00:05:06.527 --> 00:05:08.723
She didn't choose me.

00:05:08.723 --> 00:05:11.211
- This is Sugar right
here. The deep red one.

00:05:11.211 --> 00:05:12.679
- [Niki] You so pretty.

00:05:12.679 --> 00:05:15.128
Sugar needs a new pair of shoes.

00:05:15.128 --> 00:05:17.980
- Two pairs.
- Two pairs of shoes.

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:20.984
- So one of the main purposes
of trimming horses' feet

00:05:20.984 --> 00:05:22.855
is to make sure that
finger nail is trimmed back

00:05:22.855 --> 00:05:25.653
so it doesn't interfere
with proper movement.

00:05:25.653 --> 00:05:27.229
Now I've pushed the tang away from me

00:05:27.229 --> 00:05:29.023
and use only the corse side.

00:05:29.023 --> 00:05:31.272
Pulling is easier than pushing it.

00:05:31.272 --> 00:05:32.951
And you go around like
that until you shape it.

00:05:32.951 --> 00:05:34.862
And you'll see there's a white line here.

00:05:34.862 --> 00:05:37.001
You don't wanna go any
closer in than that.

00:05:37.001 --> 00:05:39.902
But that's how we're gonna do it.

00:05:39.902 --> 00:05:42.485
(upbeat muzak)

00:05:45.956 --> 00:05:46.923
- [Garrett] Been wrestling with Sugar

00:05:46.923 --> 00:05:48.336
for a little while now.

00:05:48.336 --> 00:05:50.369
It's tough working with
this gigantic animal

00:05:50.369 --> 00:05:51.906
that can kill you if it wanted to.

00:05:51.906 --> 00:05:52.781
- [Eric] Come on.

00:05:52.781 --> 00:05:54.556
- This horse doesn't even
know what's good for it.

00:05:54.556 --> 00:05:56.446
This is gonna be so much nicer.

00:05:56.446 --> 00:05:58.058
Come on.

00:05:58.058 --> 00:05:59.358
(Garrett sighs)

00:05:59.358 --> 00:06:01.025
- Alright. Hi again.

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:02.853
Hello.

00:06:04.225 --> 00:06:07.098
It's cold. I can't feel my hands.

00:06:07.098 --> 00:06:09.208
Been out here for like an hour I think,

00:06:09.208 --> 00:06:11.608
and we've done two of Sugar's feet.

00:06:11.608 --> 00:06:12.685
It's like a pedicure.

00:06:12.685 --> 00:06:14.423
- Looks like half of a coconut.

00:06:14.423 --> 00:06:15.929
- What?
- It does, doesn't it?

00:06:15.929 --> 00:06:17.412
Look like half of a coconut?

00:06:17.412 --> 00:06:18.245
- No.

00:06:18.245 --> 00:06:19.473
- I'm gonna confess something that may not

00:06:19.473 --> 00:06:20.491
be that surprising.

00:06:20.491 --> 00:06:23.123
These are my first time putting on chaps.

00:06:23.123 --> 00:06:26.047
- Well, your first problem
is pronouncing it correctly.

00:06:26.047 --> 00:06:27.931
It's 'shaps', not 'chaps'.

00:06:27.931 --> 00:06:29.447
Chaps are British dudes.

00:06:29.447 --> 00:06:32.010
- So this is to protect
us from horse nails.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:33.922
- In this case, yeah.

00:06:33.922 --> 00:06:35.754
- Can you help me?

00:06:35.754 --> 00:06:37.667
Garrett and I have just
been working all day,

00:06:37.667 --> 00:06:39.436
but we've been working
together so I think,

00:06:39.436 --> 00:06:42.037
you know, we're enjoying
each other's company.

00:06:42.037 --> 00:06:43.766
- How's that?
- Good.

00:06:43.766 --> 00:06:44.917
- [Garrett] We were doing things way

00:06:44.917 --> 00:06:46.772
outside our comfort zone
so it was really nice

00:06:46.772 --> 00:06:49.300
to have a friend check in on
me to make sure I was good.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:50.133
- How do I look?

00:06:50.133 --> 00:06:51.385
- Great. Like a million bucks.

00:06:51.385 --> 00:06:52.315
How do I look?

00:06:52.315 --> 00:06:53.993
- Fine.
- Alright.

00:06:53.993 --> 00:06:56.529
- You look like your chaps are too short.

00:06:56.529 --> 00:06:58.349
- Mine? Yeah.

00:06:58.349 --> 00:07:01.516
(bright string music)

00:07:02.772 --> 00:07:04.615
- So the trick is to drive the nail

00:07:04.615 --> 00:07:07.039
in such a way that it goes
through, gets a good chunk

00:07:07.039 --> 00:07:09.424
of the hoof without
cutting into the quick,

00:07:09.424 --> 00:07:10.881
and still enough coming out the other side

00:07:10.881 --> 00:07:12.396
that you can crimp it.

00:07:12.396 --> 00:07:15.507
- I don't have enough hands for this.

00:07:15.507 --> 00:07:17.800
(laughs) I just keep missing.

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:21.160
Oh, my hands! This is tough stuff.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:25.357
And I feel like a total weenie,
but it's rewarding, I guess.

00:07:25.357 --> 00:07:27.978
This is part of taking
care of your horses.

00:07:27.978 --> 00:07:31.632
- How ya doin'? Ready for another one.

00:07:31.632 --> 00:07:33.417
Eric said when he came out to this land

00:07:33.417 --> 00:07:34.935
he would shoe eight horses a day.

00:07:34.935 --> 00:07:37.285
A filing and nailing it in, and oh my god.

00:07:37.285 --> 00:07:39.453
It's a lot of work.

00:07:39.453 --> 00:07:41.672
You got it Niki. Keep at it.

00:07:41.672 --> 00:07:43.075
- I'm half covered in poop.

00:07:43.075 --> 00:07:45.996
I can't believe we did
so little in four hours.

00:07:45.996 --> 00:07:48.835
We gave her two shoes because
we were taking too long,

00:07:48.835 --> 00:07:49.785
and she was getting fussy.

00:07:49.785 --> 00:07:52.053
- My hands are so tired. I can't
even hold a hammer anymore.

00:07:52.053 --> 00:07:54.256
And if I swing a hammer it's
gonna fly outta my hand.

00:07:54.256 --> 00:07:56.215
If I had to do this by myself,
I don't know what I would do.

00:07:56.215 --> 00:07:59.672
I guess I would shoe a
horse with one shoe a day.

00:07:59.672 --> 00:08:03.096
(uptempo harp music)

00:08:03.096 --> 00:08:05.706
- After lunch, we oiled saddles.

00:08:05.706 --> 00:08:06.794
- That sucked.

00:08:06.794 --> 00:08:09.647
- We are going to be
doing annual maintenance

00:08:09.647 --> 00:08:11.385
on our working stock saddles.

00:08:11.385 --> 00:08:14.134
- Should I take off my cute socks?

00:08:14.134 --> 00:08:17.390
- Well you could actually use
your cute socks to oil with.

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:19.326
- I'm gonna go change my socks.

00:08:19.326 --> 00:08:20.577
- You just go ahead and put some oil

00:08:20.577 --> 00:08:23.373
on the sponge and just sponge it in.

00:08:23.373 --> 00:08:25.322
- [Niki] How do you
know you've done enough?

00:08:25.322 --> 00:08:26.703
- [Eric] Well, the oil soaks right in,

00:08:26.703 --> 00:08:28.092
but you can see the difference.

00:08:28.092 --> 00:08:29.538
- [Niki] So far it's just a lot of work

00:08:29.538 --> 00:08:30.658
with your hands. Huh?

00:08:30.658 --> 00:08:32.629
- Pretty much.
- Manual labor.

00:08:32.629 --> 00:08:34.241
- [Garrett] There's like a latitude here.

00:08:34.241 --> 00:08:35.917
I guess I'm more used to like a precision

00:08:35.917 --> 00:08:37.259
for everything you have to do,

00:08:37.259 --> 00:08:38.672
but here, there's really a latitude

00:08:38.672 --> 00:08:40.759
of like "Yeah that's
fine. Yeah that's fine."

00:08:40.759 --> 00:08:42.346
Which is what you need
to survive out here.

00:08:42.346 --> 00:08:45.744
- It is a hard life out here,
and now I totally get it

00:08:45.744 --> 00:08:48.116
when Eric says it doesn't
matter what day it is.

00:08:48.116 --> 00:08:49.506
They do what they have to do,

00:08:49.506 --> 00:08:51.262
and they do what the ranch demands.

00:08:51.262 --> 00:08:53.148
It's been like, what, a few hours now?

00:08:53.148 --> 00:08:54.583
- A couple hours anyway.
- A couple hours?

00:08:54.583 --> 00:08:56.402
I think we're done. I'm done.

00:08:56.402 --> 00:08:58.454
- I wish I were done.
- Garrett's not done.

00:08:58.454 --> 00:09:00.526
Just a lot of work!

00:09:00.526 --> 00:09:01.692
It's a lot of work.

00:09:01.692 --> 00:09:03.531
- It's a lot of work
just to be able to work.

00:09:03.531 --> 00:09:04.783
- My body is sore.

00:09:04.783 --> 00:09:06.370
When I was sittin' there
oiling the saddles,

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:08.871
I kind of noticed my leg was hurting,

00:09:08.871 --> 00:09:11.411
and I found a gnarly bruise.

00:09:11.411 --> 00:09:13.881
Here's the bruising. It's kind of blue.

00:09:13.881 --> 00:09:14.839
Pretty gross.

00:09:14.839 --> 00:09:17.030
- Today was just a grueling day,

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:18.313
and it wasn't even as grueling

00:09:18.313 --> 00:09:20.617
as it could have been because of the rain.

00:09:20.617 --> 00:09:22.778
- We still have another early
morning ahead of us tomorrow.

00:09:22.778 --> 00:09:25.168
Maybe the cows if the weather permit it.

00:09:25.168 --> 00:09:26.138
We're not done yet.

00:09:26.138 --> 00:09:26.971
Night.

00:09:28.729 --> 00:09:31.683
- Goodnight, Niki. Good
job with the horse.

00:09:31.683 --> 00:09:35.100
(laughs)
- Thanks. Night.

00:09:37.470 --> 00:09:39.835
It's the morning of Day 3.

00:09:39.835 --> 00:09:43.501
It doesn't look like it's raining
so let's go see some cows.

00:09:43.501 --> 00:09:46.771
(bright staccato string music)

00:09:46.771 --> 00:09:49.285
- What we'd like to do is
show the cows that we have

00:09:49.285 --> 00:09:51.790
some additional supplement
for them right now.

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:54.039
- Feels like a heavy med ball, you know.

00:09:54.039 --> 00:09:55.458
- [Eric] Well, you get
your exercise this way.

00:09:55.458 --> 00:09:56.581
I can guarantee you.

00:09:56.581 --> 00:09:58.238
- How many cows do we need to lure

00:09:58.238 --> 00:10:00.104
in order for this to be successful?

00:10:00.104 --> 00:10:01.721
- [Jeanne] We got fifty last time.

00:10:01.721 --> 00:10:03.967
- You're looking at probably
30 right now. You can see them.

00:10:03.967 --> 00:10:05.345
- I don't see any cows.

00:10:05.345 --> 00:10:07.060
- Two right on the horizon there?

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:08.669
- Yes. Yes, I do.
- Then on the ridge land.

00:10:08.669 --> 00:10:10.181
- Oh, okay. And then I
see a third one there.

00:10:10.181 --> 00:10:12.860
- And another one down there.
- Okay, so that's three.

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:14.277
- Heeeey coooows!

00:10:17.028 --> 00:10:18.422
- Literally, hey cows.

00:10:18.422 --> 00:10:20.450
- Yep, and I just do like
this to kind of broadcast.

00:10:20.450 --> 00:10:21.867
- Heeeey coooows!

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:27.131
- That was good.
- You should try, Garrett.

00:10:27.131 --> 00:10:28.548
- Heeeey coooows!

00:10:31.928 --> 00:10:33.569
(cow moos in distance)

00:10:33.569 --> 00:10:34.752
- [Eric] Alright, we're comin'.

00:10:34.752 --> 00:10:35.827
(mimics cow sound)

00:10:35.827 --> 00:10:36.877
- [Niki] It's like Chewbaccaca.

00:10:36.877 --> 00:10:38.522
How long do we do this for?

00:10:38.522 --> 00:10:40.075
- 'Til the come around.
- 'Til they come?

00:10:40.075 --> 00:10:41.117
- [Jeanne] This is the other lesson

00:10:41.117 --> 00:10:43.157
you get from ranching is patience.

00:10:43.157 --> 00:10:45.670
- Yeah. I'm noticing that one.

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:47.087
Heeeeey cooooows!

00:10:52.230 --> 00:10:54.645
(cow moos in distance)

00:10:54.645 --> 00:10:57.184
It's been only half an
hour, forty-five minutes

00:10:57.184 --> 00:11:00.943
of calling cows out here
and we think it's too cold,

00:11:00.943 --> 00:11:02.519
and they've kind of
settled in for the day.

00:11:02.519 --> 00:11:03.935
- You did a good job
though. You got 'em moving.

00:11:03.935 --> 00:11:04.813
You got 'em started.

00:11:04.813 --> 00:11:06.412
- Thank you. Yeah, we
got some progress made,

00:11:06.412 --> 00:11:07.698
but not a whole lot.

00:11:07.698 --> 00:11:09.750
- Thank you guys.
- Thank you.

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:10.745
- Niki? Hi.

00:11:10.745 --> 00:11:12.330
- Oh my god. This is one
of the most difficult

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:14.289
living experience I've ever had.

00:11:14.289 --> 00:11:16.085
- It took so long to get out to

00:11:16.085 --> 00:11:18.010
where the cows are, and it's so hard.

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:20.655
And you just gotta be doing
it for the good of the cows,

00:11:20.655 --> 00:11:22.583
For the good of the land, and to survive.

00:11:22.583 --> 00:11:24.507
- [Niki] Jeanne and Eric took
care of us very, very well,

00:11:24.507 --> 00:11:26.582
but taking part in a tiny part

00:11:26.582 --> 00:11:29.423
of their lives has exhausted me.

00:11:29.423 --> 00:11:30.889
It's physically challenging,

00:11:30.889 --> 00:11:33.231
but it's also mentally challenging.

00:11:33.231 --> 00:11:35.295
- I thought it was going to be Niki and I

00:11:35.295 --> 00:11:37.239
barely hanging on and kind of looking

00:11:37.239 --> 00:11:39.314
to each other for, sort of, strength,

00:11:39.314 --> 00:11:41.930
but Niki was so strong shoeing the horses

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:44.367
and calling the cattle
and doing everything.

00:11:44.367 --> 00:11:45.760
- There's no time for
Garrett and I to be annoyed

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:47.534
with each other or to piss each other off.

00:11:47.534 --> 00:11:50.120
We were shoeing a horse, and
when he couldn't do it anymore,

00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:51.150
I had to step in and do it.

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:52.273
And when I couldn't do it anymore,

00:11:52.273 --> 00:11:53.768
he would step back in and do it.

00:11:53.768 --> 00:11:55.922
- At the end of the day, we
were just there for each other.

00:11:55.922 --> 00:11:57.790
This landscape can get really lonely

00:11:57.790 --> 00:11:59.534
so it was great to have her there.

00:11:59.534 --> 00:12:01.165
- It's only been three days

00:12:01.165 --> 00:12:03.051
and I feel like my body's falling apart.

00:12:03.051 --> 00:12:04.662
- It's so hard to live out here,

00:12:04.662 --> 00:12:07.503
and I have so much respect
for the people who do it.

00:12:07.503 --> 00:12:09.416
- [Niki] Say bye to the Africa Room.

00:12:09.416 --> 00:12:10.999
- Bye, Africa Room.

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:14.501
(Niki laughs)

00:12:14.501 --> 00:12:17.342
- Next week
- On Home Buddies...

00:12:17.342 --> 00:12:18.259
- Earth ships.

00:12:18.259 --> 00:12:19.092
- [Garrett] It sounds a
little high highfalutin

00:12:19.092 --> 00:12:20.104
and hippie-dippy.

00:12:20.104 --> 00:12:21.738
There's a garage with, like, plants in it.

00:12:21.738 --> 00:12:23.976
- I think they grow their own vegetables?

00:12:23.976 --> 00:12:25.389
- That's kale.
- I hear birds?

00:12:25.389 --> 00:12:27.023
- There's birds.
- There's birds?

00:12:27.023 --> 00:12:28.361
This is fascinating.

00:12:28.361 --> 00:12:30.450
- [Garrett] We're doing a pretty bad job,

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:31.701
but we're learning.

00:12:31.701 --> 00:12:34.034
(hip muzak)

